    {
      "slug": "how-much-ram-do-you-need-2026",
      "title": "How much RAM do you actually need in 2026?",
      "tag": "Memory",
      "read_minutes": 6,
      "published_on": "2026-03-18",
      "image": "assets/art-ram.svg",
      "excerpt": "Where 16GB still holds up, and when the jump to 32GB is worth paying for.",
      "body": "For most people buying a new desktop in 2026, **32GB of DDR5 is the sensible default**, and 16GB is still workable if the budget is tight.\n\n## 16GB: enough for everyday use and most games\nBrowsing, office work, streaming and the majority of current games run comfortably in 16GB. The limit shows up when you stack workloads: a game plus a browser with dozens of tabs plus voice chat can push a 16GB system into swapping to disk, which is when stutters appear.\n\n## 32GB: headroom for heavier games and multitasking\nNewer open-world games increasingly use more than 16GB on their own, and creative work such as photo editing, 4K video timelines or running virtual machines benefits directly from more memory. The price gap between 16GB and 32GB kits has narrowed enough that 32GB is usually the better long-term buy.\n\n## 64GB and beyond\nOnly worth it for specific workloads: large video projects, 3D rendering, big datasets or several virtual machines at once.\n\n## Capacity matters more than speed\nRunning out of memory hurts far more than slightly slower memory. On current AMD and Intel platforms DDR5-6000 is a good target; beyond that, gains in games are small. Always buy a matched kit of two modules so the memory runs in dual-channel mode."
    },
    {
      "slug": "gpu-vram-explained",
      "title": "GPU VRAM explained",
      "tag": "Graphics",
      "read_minutes": 8,
      "published_on": "2026-03-04",
      "image": "assets/art-gpu.svg",
      "excerpt": "What video memory does, how much your resolution needs, and what it does not fix.",
      "body": "VRAM (video memory) is the graphics card's own high-speed memory. It holds everything the GPU needs close at hand while drawing a frame: textures, geometry and the frame buffers themselves.\n\n## What uses VRAM\nTexture quality is the biggest consumer, followed by resolution and features such as ray tracing and frame generation. Higher resolutions need larger frame buffers, and higher texture settings load more detailed assets.\n\n## What happens when you run out\nThe card has to fetch data from system RAM over the much slower PCIe bus. The result is usually not a lower average frame rate but stutter, texture pop-in, or textures that stay blurry.\n\n## How much do you need?\n- **1080p:** 8GB works, though some recent games need texture settings lowered.\n- **1440p:** 12GB is a comfortable target.\n- **4K:** 16GB or more.\n\n## What VRAM does not fix\nMore memory does not make a slower GPU faster. A card with 16GB and a weaker chip will still lose to a stronger 12GB card whenever memory is not the bottleneck. Compare real performance first, then check that the memory is enough for your resolution."
    },
    {
      "slug": "choosing-a-cpu",
      "title": "What matters most when choosing a CPU?",
      "tag": "Processors",
      "read_minutes": 7,
      "published_on": "2026-02-20",
      "image": "assets/art-cpu.svg",
      "excerpt": "Cores, clocks, cache and power draw — ranked by how much they change daily use.",
      "body": "Spec sheets list a lot of numbers. Here they are, ranked by how much they change day-to-day use.\n\n## 1. Platform and upgrade path\nThe socket decides which motherboards and future CPUs you can use. AMD's AM5 supports DDR5 only; Intel's LGA1700 supports both DDR4 and DDR5. Check the platform before comparing anything else.\n\n## 2. Performance in your workload\nGames favour fast individual cores and large caches, which is why chips with extra cache, such as AMD's X3D models, do so well in gaming. Rendering, compiling code and video export scale with core count instead.\n\n## 3. Core count\nSix modern cores are enough for gaming. Eight or more help if you stream, edit video or run heavy multitasking.\n\n## 4. Power and cooling\nTDP is a guideline, not a measurement. Look at the maximum package power: a chip that can draw 250 W needs a much stronger cooler and power supply than one that tops out under 100 W.\n\n## 5. Clock speed\nUseful only for comparing chips of the same generation and brand. Across different architectures, the clock number on its own says little about real performance."
    },
    {
      "slug": "ssd-vs-hdd",
      "title": "SSD vs HDD: what's the difference?",
      "tag": "Storage",
      "read_minutes": 5,
      "published_on": "2026-02-06",
      "image": "assets/art-ssd.svg",
      "excerpt": "Speed, lifespan and cost per terabyte, and where each type still makes sense.",
      "body": "## How they work\nA hard disk drive (HDD) stores data on spinning magnetic platters read by a moving head. A solid-state drive (SSD) stores data on flash memory chips and has no moving parts.\n\n## Speed\nThis is the headline difference. A typical HDD reads around 150–250 MB/s. A SATA SSD reaches about 550 MB/s, and an NVMe SSD on PCIe 4.0 can exceed 7,000 MB/s. Random access, where hard drives are weakest, matters even more than sequential speed: it is why an SSD makes a system boot, open applications and load games so much faster.\n\n## Lifespan and reliability\nSSDs are rated in terabytes written (TBW). A typical 1TB drive is rated for several hundred TBW, far more than most home users will ever write. Hard drives have moving parts, so they are more sensitive to knocks and mechanical wear.\n\n## Cost per terabyte\nHard drives still win here, especially above 4TB.\n\n## Which should you buy?\nUse an NVMe SSD for your operating system, applications and games. Add a hard drive only if you need cheap bulk storage for media archives or backups."
    }
